# md2c

Publish a Markdown directory tree to Confluence as ADF pages. CLI: `atbp md2c`.

## Stack

- ZIO + better-files; PlantUML for `plantuml*` fenced blocks; Mermaid
  (mermaid-java / GraalJS) for `mermaid*` fenced blocks; D2 (the `d2` CLI, on
  `PATH`) for `d2*` fenced blocks
- Atlassian ADF via `adf-builder` (adf-builder-java + markdown + jackson2)
- Confluence Cloud REST via `confluence.Client` (md2c does not HTTP itself)
- zio-config: HOCON (`.md2c.conf`, user `atbp` conf), YAML (front matter)

Depends on: `adf-builder`, `confluence`. Wired from `cli`.

## Commands

```bash
sbt --client "md2c/compile"
sbt --client "md2c/test"
sbt --client "md2c/testOnly *SourceTreeSpec*"
sbt --client "md2c/testOnly *ParserSpec*"
sbt --client "md2c/testOnly *StagedTreeSpec*"
sbt --client "md2c/testOnly *MermaidSpec*"
sbt --client "md2c/testOnly *D2Spec*"
sbt --client fixup
```

Live publish needs Confluence creds in user `atbp` config. `PublisherSpec` is
`TestAspect.ignore` — do not un-ignore unless running against a real site.

`D2Spec`'s Group B tests need a real `d2` binary and are guarded by
`.whenZIO(d2Available)`; when `d2` isn't on `PATH` they report as **ignored**,
not failed and not silently absent — that ignored count is the only signal
distinguishing "D2 verified" from "D2 not verified" on a given run.

## Pipeline

`SourceTree` → reserve page IDs → convert and publish one page at a time

1. Walk up from the given dir until `.md2c.conf` (HOCON `spaceKey`, `pageId`)
2. Assign Confluence titles for the whole page set (`PageTitles.assign`) **before**
   any draft: unique logical titles unchanged; each duplicate set sorted by source
   path—first path bare, later paths +N U+200B (ZERO WIDTH SPACE). Then match
   a direct child by that title. If none matches, reuse a page anywhere in the
   space with that exact title and move it under this parent on publish; a
   current page is not moved under a draft parent. Otherwise create an empty
   draft. Read titles without converting Markdown and retain metadata without
   bodies
3. Visit children before parents, converting only the current page via
   `StagedTree.Page.convertPage`: resolve document links, parse MD → ADF, then
   PlantUML, Mermaid, D2, and Extensions; drop Data nodes and empty dirs
4. Update pages; attach media; skip if up to date; discard completed bodies.
   Publishing uses `withParallelism(1)` to bound per-page renderers and uploads too.
   `StagedTree.from` still materializes a whole tree for callers that need one;
   do not use it in the publisher

`spaceKey`: source conf, else user `md2c.Conf`. `pageId`: source conf only.

## Conventions

- Folder notes: `{dir}/{dir}.md` or `README.md` or `index.md` → `MarkdownBranch`
- Dotfiles skipped in the walk (`.md2c.conf` is loaded separately, not a page)
- Title = YAML `title:` else node name; strip a leading H1 that duplicates title.
  Duplicate logical titles: Confluence title at publish boundary only (child match,
  space-wide reuse, else `createDraft`); first path in sort order bare, later
  paths +N U+200B. A reused page keeps its id and is not deleted from the old
  parent. A changed suffix count recreates the page and deletes the unmatched
  child. Never write U+200B
  into Markdown, link text, or content hashes—`StagedTree.Page.title` stays logical
- Front matter must start at byte 0 with `---\n`
- Content hash is SHA-1 of the markdown **body** (stored in version.message),
  combined with resolved document-link URLs when present; a destination page ID
  change republishes incoming links. A title-only front-matter change does **not**
  republish the page itself
- `DocumentLinks` resolves relative links to source paths in the published tree,
  before ADF conversion (which drops relative links). It uses CommonMark's AST
  with adf-builder's extensions, then `MarkdownParser.children` for ADF conversion.
  Link text, title, formatting, query and fragment are preserved
- Directory-only pages get `Extensions.childPages`; extra Confluence children
  and attachments not in the source tree are deleted
- New pages are created as `draft`; existing pages matched by title
- Images: markdown media → `ExternalMedia` → attach → rewrite to `fileMedia`;
  attachment `comment` is `file.sha1`. Percent-encoded relative image URLs are
  decoded before the file is resolved; a literal `%20` path is not the filename
- `plantuml*` and `mermaid*` fences (`mermaid`, `mermaid.png`,
  `mermaid.svg`) render in `PlantUml.scala` / `Mermaid.scala`; tests in
  `MermaidSpec`
- Mermaid success (`JavaMermaid.renderDetailed` → `isSuccessful`) →
  `MediaSingle` / `ExternalMedia` (same staging pattern as PlantUML)
- Mermaid soft failure (`!isSuccessful`) → keep the mermaid `CodeBlock`
  (original language); body =
  `%% Mermaid rendering failed. See details below.` + newline + original
  source; immediate sibling `CodeBlock` language `text`, body =
  `Mermaid failure details:` + newline + `getErrorMessage()`; staging
  still succeeds
- `d2*` fences (`d2`, `d2.png`, `d2.svg`) render in `D2.scala` by shelling out
  to the `d2` CLI (`d2 --stdout-format <png|svg> - -`); tests in `D2Spec`.
  Sibling-insertion for the failure path is shared with Mermaid via
  `DiagramFailure.scala`
- D2 success → `MediaSingle` / `ExternalMedia` (same staging pattern as
  Mermaid), decided by exit code alone (d2 prints a `success:` line to
  stderr on every successful run, so stderr content never decides failure)
- D2 soft failure (bad source, a timeout, `d2` unusable or missing from
  `PATH`) → keep the `d2*` `CodeBlock` (original language); body =
  `# D2 rendering failed. See details below.` + newline + original source;
  immediate sibling `CodeBlock` language `text`, body =
  `D2 failure details:` + newline + details — d2's own stderr for bad
  source, or exactly `Could not render diagram: d2 not on PATH` when the
  binary is absent from `PATH`; staging still succeeds
- `Could not render diagram: d2 not on PATH` is reserved for a binary that
  really is absent (`D2.onPath`): `start()` reports a missing binary, a
  missing exec bit and a refused `fork` all as the same `IOException`, so
  when the executable *does* resolve the OS error is reported verbatim
  instead — a broken install must not read as an uninstalled one
- a `d2` that exits without draining stdin breaks the pipe on sources over
  the OS pipe buffer (~64KB); that write error is swallowed so d2's own exit
  code and stderr decide the outcome, because failing the `Task` there would
  abort the whole run over one diagram
- concurrent `d2` processes are capped run-wide by a module-level semaphore
  in `D2.scala` (`availableProcessors`), not `withParallelism`: `StagedTree`
  converts pages in parallel, so a per-page bound leaves the tree-wide
  process count unbounded. Each distinct `RenderKey` renders exactly once,
  so duplicate fences share one file and cannot overwrite each other
- Thrown exceptions still fail the `Task` (no catch-and-fallback)
- ADF `transformDescendants` mutates in place — do not assume a copy
- `build.sbt` keeps hidden `.md2c.conf` visible in tests via
  `Test / fileInputExcludeFilter`

## Boundaries

- Unit tests stay offline. Fixtures: `md2c/src/test/resources/{trees,markdown,publisher}`
- Do not parse Markdown by hand — `MarkdownParser` from adf-builder-java-markdown
- Confluence HTTP stays in `confluence`, not md2c; diagram rendering stays in
  md2c (no HTTP, no Node)
- `--cleanup` deletes the **current user's drafts in the whole space**
- Data files are not pages; they attach only when referenced as media
- `d2` must be on `PATH` for D2 rendering; md2c does **not** check its
  version (deliberate — a bad native install surfaces d2's own stderr
  through the soft-failure path, which is more informative than any version
  message md2c could synthesize). The published container image pins
  `Versions.D2` (`project/Dependencies.scala`) and installs that exact
  build, which is the de facto compatibility guarantee for image users
